---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DocxToMarkdown from '@/components/tools/data/DocxToMarkdown.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="docx-to-markdown">
  <DocxToMarkdown client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert Word to Markdown</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your <code>.docx</code> files. You can add several at once and switch between them.</li>
      <li>Pick your Markdown dialect: ATX or setext headings, hyphen/asterisk/plus bullets, fenced or indented code, underscore or asterisk emphasis.</li>
      <li>Decide about images. Off keeps the file small; on puts each picture inline as a base64 data URI, so the Markdown is self-contained.</li>
      <li>Review and download. The second tab shows the intermediate HTML, so you can trace any unexpected result.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the conversion works</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion has two stages, and they explain most results. First, <strong>mammoth</strong>
      unzips the <code>.docx</code> and maps the semantic styles of Word onto HTML: “Heading 2” becomes
      <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>, a list paragraph becomes <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, a hyperlink becomes
      <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>. Then <strong>Turndown</strong> converts that HTML into Markdown with the style
      options you chose. Both stages run in this browser tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mammoth maps styles, not appearance. A line that only looks like a heading because it is 18pt and
      bold is still a paragraph, so it becomes body text. If your headings are missing, apply real heading
      styles in Word and convert again. This usually takes two minutes, and it also makes the document
      accessible.
    </p>

    <h2>Element mapping</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-max border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">In Word</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">In Markdown</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Heading 1-6, Title, Subtitle</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>#</code> … <code>######</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Bulleted list, including nesting</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>-</code> with two-space indents</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Numbered list</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>1.</code>, <code>2.</code>, …</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Bold / italic / strikethrough</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>**bold**</code>, <code>_italic_</code>, <code>~~struck~~</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Hyperlink</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>[text](url)</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Quote / Intense Quote style</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>&gt; blockquote</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Table</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">GitHub pipe table with an alignment row</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Inline code character style</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2"><code>`code`</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Fonts, colours, point sizes</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Dropped: Markdown has no equivalent</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Footnotes, comments, tracked changes</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Not converted</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Tables</h2>
    <p>
      Word tables become GitHub-flavoured pipe tables. The first row becomes the header, as almost all real
      documents intend, and cell alignment goes into the divider row as <code>:---</code>,
      <code>---:</code> or <code>:---:</code>. Line breaks in a cell become <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>, because
      a Markdown table row cannot span lines, and pipe characters in the text are escaped so the columns
      stay intact. Merged cells cannot be shown, so a merged cell gives its value once and the covered
      positions stay empty.
    </p>

    <h2>About images</h2>
    <p>
      Word stores pictures inside the document package, so there is no URL to link to. There are two
      options: embed each image as a base64 data URI, which keeps everything in one file but can make it
      several times larger, or leave images out. Embedding is off by default, and the tool warns you when
      the result is larger than a megabyte. A change to this setting reads the documents again, because
      mammoth makes the decision while it reads the file.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Moving documentation from Word into a Git repository, a wiki or a static site generator.</li>
      <li>Turning a brief from a client into content for a CMS that uses Markdown.</li>
      <li>Making documents diffable, so changes between versions can be reviewed in a pull request.</li>
      <li>Batch-migrating a folder of specifications: drop them all and download one ZIP.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
